#import "../../shared/functions.typ" : *

= Crisial Kingdom <crisial-kingdom>

#flush-block[
- *Population:* Roughly 200,000 humans, gwerin, halflings, and dwarves.
- *Major Cities:* Crisial City (#sym.approx 30,000), Beiand (#sym.approx 15,000), Inegand (#sym.approx 10,000), Honel (#sym.approx 8,000). Most of the dwarves live near Honel, with gwerin in the capital and parts east.
- *Names and Architecture:* Use a mix of traditional English and Welsh. Architecture is late medieval fantasy Europe.
- *Economics:* Trade is the major thing. Extraction near Honel, farming throughout. Rule of law is very strong. Acts most like a modern semi-democracy.
- *Magics:* An even mix. Druid grove at Lady's Grove, arcane school in the capital, as well as major temples throughout.
- *Religion:* Pluralistic. Worship who, where, and what you want, as long as it's not demons.
]

Crisial is the "shining light" nation (or so they like to portray themselves). Founded by the Catalysts (the otherworldly Vassarian, the calculating and political Paresten, the quiet anchor Kalesin, and the tree-friend Safrir) in the early 200s, it is currently ruled by the time-lost aelvar wizard Raanee, first of her name. It is the home to the majority of the gwerin of western Noefra, but also counts many humans, dwarves, halflings, and others. Magically powerful, it is also a dominant trading nation, occupying as it does the crossroads between the eastern nations and the western powerhouses. There are functioning independent courts, and rule of law is observed. Even the Queen does not have the independent power of high justice except in matters of emergency.

Governmentally, it is a classical feudal nation, although with strong traditions of yeomanry and independence. Each of the major cities and their surroundings is governed by a Duke, and the Queen and her court handles the capital and the surroundings and mediates between Dukes. She acts more like a head of state in a confederation than an autocrat. Other major political forces are the various temples (it is a devout but multi-creedal nation) and the High Houses of the gwerin, which have aristocratic status. 

One key strength is the Four Towers, a pre-Cataclysm library and research facility located on an island about 10 miles north of Crisial City. This is the headquarters for the Sages Guild, the predominant academic research organization in western Noefra, although the Sages have outposts in most significant cities across the region. This makes Crisial a magical powerhouse and the home of many of the more powerful arcanists and divine workers. It has a druidic grove (the Ladysgrove) located outside the city, formerly headed by Safrir of the Catalysts until his death in 254 AC.

== Borders and Extent
Crisial Kingdom is bordered by the Sundown Range to the east and south, the goblin lands (formerly the Windwalker Confederacy) to the south and west, and Wyrmhold to the west. It at least notionally controls most of the southern Lupaus Plain (other than the section to the north controlled by the isolationist Seraaja) and Southshore regions. Lake Coy'in forms a natural border to the north. Of all the nations of western Noefra, Crisial is the largest in land area. Much of that area is only sparsely populated, if at all, and the existing population is spread along a narrow strip that runs the entire width.

Two-thirds of the population lives between Crisial City and Rauviz along the shores of Lake Coy'in, with another sixth down the Bor's Head river to Honel, and the remainder split between settlements along the western Dragon Road and the south-western Goblin Road. Most of the out-country is inhabited only by independant hamlets and villages, many of whom pre-date the re-settlement from Kaelthia and who only grudgingly (if at all) accept guidance from the Crown. Those of the former Horse Clans who were willing to cooperate with society concentrate along the Khol river from Vaeltaa up to Pyhin on the shore of the lake, although their pastoral ways mean their habitations are not fixed in place but wander with the seasons. The total land area of the kingdom is 215,000 sq. mi., but 90% of its population lies within an area of 64,000 sq. mi., 53,000 of that lying within a 90 miles of the lake-shore.

== Demographics

The population of the Crisial Kingdom as of 260 AC is about 200,000 individuals. A strong plurality of them (roughly 48%) are wall-builder #safe-link("humans", "humans"), while #safe-link("gwerin", "gwerin") (17%), halflings (15%), dwarves (12%), goblinoids (6%), and others (2%) round out the remainder. There is substantial geographic separation among the minorities, however---gwerin mostly live in and around Crisial City and Beiand in central Southshore, halflings are concentrated in the north-eastern corner near Rauviz, dwarves in and around Honel, and goblins to the south-west.

== Government Structure

The head of government is Queen Raanee I, a younger elven wizard and former adventurer. She was born shortly before Moon's Fall, but was preserved from that catastrophe in temporal stasis due to the sacrifice of her parents. Found by the adventuring group called the Dimwit Syndicate in the southern Jungle of Fangs in 212 AC, she spent a few decades adventuring as a wizard before accepting the position of Queen of the newly formed Crisial Kingdom after the breakup of the Council system in 238 AC. Chosen mostly as a non-political candidate who could unite the gwerin High Houses but who could also command the respect of the human majority, she has ruled ably for the last 22 years. Now at the (biological) age of 72, she is in her prime. Raanee has not married, although she has openly dallied with multiple men of several lineages, albeit without issue. Creating an heir is, for most of the nobles, _the_ most pressing issue.

Raanee is an idealist and optimist who believes that people are mostly good and that the role of government is mostly to help them be free to pursue happiness. She's not totally naive, however, and has instituted a fairly thorough Inspectorate to investigate and prosecute bad actors among the elite nobility. She also understands the threats posed by the post-Federated Nations order, and has been actively working to build up defensive forces, both in the form of trained citizen militias and a small standing army called the Ranger Corps. She is hampered by tax-averse citizenry, as well as the sheer size of the kingdom. The high-roads are patrolled well, as are the major cities. There is an active effort to come to the aid of the smaller towns and villages, but the Rangers are few in number and spread across the land. As a result, adventurers are often called to provide services.

=== The Dukes
Each of the four major cities (Beiand, Inegand, Honel, and Vaeltaa) and the surrounding countryside are under the control of the Dukes, nobles originally chosen when the form of government was voted on in 238 AC. Together with the Queen and the Mayor of Crisial City, they form the Privy Council that shares responsibility for governing the nation. Dukes are responsible for maintaining order and roads and mediating civil disputes and minor crimes. Major crimes are referred to the Crown Courts; Dukes do not have independent powers of High Justice (ie capital punishment). Dukes levy taxes on their populace and are responsible for about 80% of the Crown Budget; the remainder comes from levies on adventuring wealth, Crown Charters for larger organizations, and border fees on merchants. By and large, taxes are low and primarily come as excise taxes on goods.

Much of the nation is outside the purview of any of the Dukes, however. The majority of it, in fact. But almost all of the population lies in their zones of authority.

The Duke of Beiand is the Matriarch of Riain, Bronwyn sur Riain. Her house has rejected most of the hidebound gender roles of the gwerin and they are firm royalists and supporters of the Queen. Her domain covers from the eastern gate of Crisial City all the way to Borderton and includes most of the population.

The Duke of Honel is Elspeth Margadh-Shoryu, a younger dwarven matron. She and her husband Tomor don't rule as much as they mediate among the clans of dwarves and authority-allergic local trappers, settlers, and herders. Most of those in her care moved to the foothills along the southern edge of the nation to get away from civilization. Her domain is by far the smallest, both in area and in population, but provides an outsized number of Rangers. She is firmly loyal to the Queen.

The Duke of Inegand is Rufus Redhousen, a middle-aged human male. Until the start of the Time of Troubles, he was extremely wealthy as his city benefited from both the trade through Fort Hope as well as trade with Wyrmhold. But the Time of Troubles saw two major changes---the fall of Fort Hope when the goblin lands closed off, as well as the peace treaty with Pyhin, which opened trade along the river to the Lake. Lord Inegand's domain has, as a result, suffered. This has caused him to grumble, but so far he hasn't shown any signs of looking elsewhere.

The Duke of Vaeltaa really isn't a Duke. Vaeltaa, after the fall of Vetel and the liberation of Pyhin, has become the summer home of the Hevonen, those of the Seraaja who were open to trade with the outside. Their young leader Paeter holds court in the border trade city, but mostly to assure the wary merchants that they are not unprotected. His lands are only very lightly settled, with only Vaeltaa itself and Siltford, a small river town located when the Dragon Road crosses the Drina River and joins with the Valford road.

The title of Mayor of Crisial City, currently the First Seat of House Arlwyd Geoffrey sur Arlwydd, has the smallest domain (just the city and its immediate environs), but is routinely considered the second-ranking noble of the nation. His house are inventors and traders, having invented the cold-box, a magical device capable of preserving perishables for long trips via ship or wagon. He and the Queen have a complex relationship and do not always see eye to eye; he is an arch conservative and pragmatist.

=== Minor Nobles
Those who either own large-enough portions of productive land, mines, manufacturing centers, or other significant economic contributions can apply to become a minor noble. They get a House Name and a title (usually Sir/Madam), as well as access to petition the Privy Council directly (instead of having to bring the matter before the local Duke first). In return, they pay a yearly tax based on their holdings or income, although many are allowed to pay in services such as arming militias or providing free transport for government goods and maintaining roads. This title must be renewed upon the death of the holder; it doesn't pass down automatically.

The heads of the Gwerin Houses (high and low) are all counted as hereditary minor nobles with only the most cursory check when it passes to the heir. This was part of the grand bargain struck to gain buy-in from the hierarchical gwerin society.

=== The Crown Court
Crisial Kingdom is unique among the nations of Western Noefra in that the judiciary is independent of both the Crown and the Dukes. It exists at three levels---the local, the regional, and the High Court at Crisial City. Local free-holders nominate their local judges, usually two of them sitting individually, who hear minor criminal cases as well as many civil cases, but cannot hand down any sentence that would strip someone of their entire possessions, exile them, or result in a sentence of death or imprisonment of more than a few days. Local judges in a region (roughly a Dukedom) nominate the regional judges, serving as a panel of five. Regional judges must be confirmed by a priest of Ytra. Regional judges can pass sentence of exile or long-duration imprisonment, but sentences of death or exile are automatically appealed to the High Court. The regional judges nominate four members of the High Court, who are confirmed by the High Priestess of Ytra, who serves as the Chief Judge. The High Court hears all capital cases and passes judgement.

Prosecution is done by the victim, if possible, and cases are short. The emphasis is on doing justice, not upholding the letter of the law. No one is above the law, but Dukes and the Queen have the right to have their cases heard at the regional level instead of the local one. In principle, the High Court could depose the Queen. That would get messy, however.

=== The Free Hold
Crisial Kingdom has an established system to encourage its citizens to strike out and break new ground (literally). It maintains a property register in each region, and actively surveys the surroundings of each settlement. Any person who registers a claim on unclaimed land and lives on it, developing it to specific levels, can claim Free Hold on that ground. Unlike many of the other feudal areas, free-holders own their land outright, rather than leasing it from nobles. The catch is that free-holders must actively maintain their land and roads and provide people for militias, and are held to a higher standard of legal dealing. Similarly, those who are inclined to invention or mercantile efforts can apply for Crown Charters, acting somewhat like a cross between a company charter and a patent. Those who Charter an invention must allow others to use it under fair and reasonable license terms. Free-holders are the backbone of Crisial; their independence and cooperation is key in keeping the oversized nation together.

== Regions and Settlements
Scholars usually split Southshore and the Lupaus Plains, which are the geographic regions of the Crisial Kingdom, into smaller sub-regions.
=== Southshore Proper
The actual Southshore region is a narrow, fertile strip of plains stretching about 60 miles away from the south end of Lake Coy'in from Kaela river around to the Khol on the west side of the lake. About 80% of the population of Crisial Kingdom lives in this region, most of them east of the Bor's Head river mouth, where Crisial City itself lies. The east is mostly human, halfling, and gwerin, while the west becomes progressively more human and goblin.

*Settlements:*
- Crisial City (#sym.approx 30,000). Capital and closest point to the Four Towers.
- Beiand (#sym.approx 15,000). Gateway to the east. Widely seen as the breadbasket of the kingdom.
- Inegand (#sym.approx 10,000). Trade city and original gateway to the west. Now reduced in prestige.
- Pyhin (#sym.approx 6,000, including 2,000 or so gnomes). Holy city of the Seraaja and home of the Puuha, secretive gnome-folk.
- Egoriden (#sym.approx 4,000). Notable for being located outside of the Crystal Spire.
- Siltford (#sym.approx 3,000). An up-and-coming town located where the Dragon Road crosses the Drina river and the Valford Road, which had paralleled that river, joins the Dragon Road. With the closing of the South Road, much of the trade from Byssia and Byarmarsh comes up that path.
- Westerwich (#sym.approx 2,000). Mostly founded to supply a lighthouse due to rocky coasts.

*Other Points of Interest* include
- The Crystal Spire, an ancient tower that stretches into the heavens and contains monsters, almost as if it were designed to test would-be heroes. Built in the First Age and holding too many floors to count, each party that ventures inside finds something different. Overseen by House Tuathail of the gwerin, who remain abjectly neutral politically.
- The Towers of Sun, Earth, Moon, and Stars (usually called the Four Towers), a pre-Cataclysm gwerin research facility and library. Home of the Sages Guild, it is one of the premier schools of magic and history, especially since the destruction of the Academy at Kaelthia. Maintained by House Hawal of the gwerin High Houses, who also remain quite neutral politically.

=== Sundown Hills
The region south and east of Southshore Proper is the Sundown Hills. This area contains little in the way of large settlements, and the villages and hamlets that hide in the rolling terrain are happy to keep it that way. They only grudgingly accept the authority of the Kingdom, preferring the occasional visit of an Oathbound assigned to the High Court, who acts as a neutral arbiter for the few disputes they show to outsiders.

=== Bor's Sacrifice
Nestled in the southern hills at the source of the Bor's Head river due south of Crisial City is the region known as Bor's Sacrifice, after an early adventurer who gave his life to clear out a nest of demon-tainted goblins. This is where most of the nation's dwarves live, as well as where almost all of the operational mines lie. The inhabitants here are hardy and independant. Their Duke has little to do with actually ruling and spends her time trying to herd proverbial cats #aside[I think that gives cats a bad name --ed] and trying to keep the various villages from feuding.

*Settlements:*
- Honel (#sym.approx 7,500). Regional capital and only real city. Half-buried in the hills.
- Adair (#sym.approx 1,500). Only other significant town.

=== Goblinward
West of Bor's Sacrifice, stretching from the southern edge of Southshore to the hills that mark the unofficial border with the (now isolationist) goblin tribes of the Windwalk and from the Bor's Head west to the Wyrmhold border (roughly marked by the Drina River for most of the way) lies the vast, mostly unsettled Goblinward. The only settlements here are along the Valford road at the far western edge; the settlements that lay along the old South Road (heading to what was Fort Hope) have mostly been abandoned in the Time of Troubles.

This area is almost entirely lawless. Only Valford maintains any significant Crown presence, and that is barely more than a platoon of Rangers and a few judges.

*Settlements:*
- Valford (#sym.approx 1,000). Trade town on the Valford road from the Tri-nation Border region. Carries most of the Byssian trade.

=== Lupaus
Along the shors of the Khol and to its north stretch the broad, grassy Lupaus Plain. Once inhabited by the mysterious and xenophobic Seraaja Vetelnaya, the area is now (somewhat) open to outsiders. The Hevonen, formerly called the Horse Clans, still range the plains raising the best horses in Western Noefra, and prefer to avoid outlanders, but they no longer attack on sight.

This area, anchored by Vaeltaa, is only weakly bound to the rest of the nation. Were it not for the close personal relationships between the Duke of Vaeltaa and the Queen, Vaeltaa and the Plain would have broken off to become independent. Of course, most there realize that doing so would have resulted in Wyrmhold absorbing them; it has tried before and would desperately love to control the entire Lupaus Plain and have an outlet to Lake Coy'in.

*Settlements:*
- Vaeltaa (#sym.approx 5,000). Trade town and gateway to Wyrmhold.
